<subsection xml:id="subsec-harmonic-oscillator">
  <title>Application: The Harmonic Oscillator</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      The techniques developed in this section apply immediately to one of the
      most important mechanical systems in engineering: the
      <term>mass-spring-damper</term> system (also called the
      <em>harmonic oscillator</em>).
      Beyond its direct engineering relevance — suspension systems, vibration
      isolation, earthquake engineering — it is the canonical model for any
      physical system exhibiting oscillatory behaviour near an equilibrium.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <!-- ─── Physical setup ─── -->

  <example xml:id="ex-spring-pendulum-setup">
    <title>The Mass-Spring-Damper System</title>
    <p>
      A mass <m>m > 0</m> is attached to a fixed support by a spring
      (stiffness <m>k > 0</m>) and a viscous damper (damping coefficient
      <m>\alpha \geq 0</m>).  The mass can move vertically; let <m>y(t)</m>
      denote its displacement from the natural rest position.
    </p>
    <figure xml:id="fig-spring-damper-diagram">
      <caption>
        Schematic of the mass-spring-damper system.
        The mass <m>m</m> hangs from a spring (stiffness <m>k</m>) and moves
        through a viscous liquid (damping coefficient <m>\alpha</m>).
        Positive <m>y</m> points downward.
      </caption>
      <image xml:id="image-spring-damper-diagram" width="40%">
        <latex-image>
          \begin{tikzpicture}[
            spring/.style={decorate,
              decoration={coil, aspect=0.4, segment length=5pt,
                          amplitude=5pt}},
            thick
          ]
            % Fixed wall (top)
            \fill[gray!40] (-1.5,4) rectangle (1.5,4.2);
            \draw[thick] (-1.5,4) -- (1.5,4);

            % Spring
            \draw[spring] (0,4) -- (0,2.35);
            \node[right] at (0.25, 3.15) {$k$};
            \draw[thick] (0,2.35) -- (0,1.9);

            % Viscous liquid cylinder
            \fill[cyan!18] (-0.95,2.05) rectangle (0.95,0.05);
            \draw[cyan!55!black, thick] (-0.95,2.05) -- (-0.95,0.05);
            \draw[cyan!55!black, thick] (0.95,2.05) -- (0.95,0.05);
            \draw[cyan!55!black, thick] (-0.95,0.05)
              arc[start angle=180,end angle=360,x radius=0.95,y radius=0.18];
            \draw[cyan!55!black, thick] (-0.95,2.05)
              arc[start angle=180,end angle=360,x radius=0.95,y radius=0.18];
            \draw[cyan!55!black, dashed] (0.95,2.05)
              arc[start angle=0,end angle=180,x radius=0.95,y radius=0.18];
            \node[cyan!45!black, right] at (0.95,1.0) {$\alpha$};

            % Mass moving in the liquid
            \fill[blue!20] (-0.5,1.15) rectangle (0.5,1.75);
            \draw[thick, blue!65!black] (-0.5,1.15) rectangle (0.5,1.75);
            \node at (0, 1.45) {$m$};

            % Rest position indicator
            \draw[dashed, gray] (-1.15,1.45) -- (1.35,1.45);
            \node[right, gray] at (1.35,1.45) {\small rest};

            % Displacement arrow
            \draw[->, red!80!black, thick] (1.45,1.45) -- (1.45,0.55);
            \node[right, red!80!black] at (1.5,1.0) {$y(t)$};
          \end{tikzpicture}
        </latex-image>
      </image>
    </figure>
    <p>
      Newton's second law gives
      <me>
        m\, y''(t) = -k\, y(t) - \alpha\, y'(t) + F(t),
      </me>
      which, after dividing by <m>m</m> and rearranging, becomes
      <men xml:id="eq-harmonic-oscillator">
        y''(t) + \underbrace{\frac{\alpha}{m}}_{2\beta}\, y'(t)
        + \underbrace{\frac{k}{m}}_{\omega_0^2}\, y(t)
        = \frac{F(t)}{m}.
      </men>
      Physical reasoning constrains the parameters:
      <m>2\beta = \alpha/m \geq 0</m> (damping is non-negative) and
      <m>\omega_0^2 = k/m > 0</m> (the spring always restores).
      Here <m>\omega_0</m> is called the <term>natural frequency</term>.
    </p>
  </example>

  <!-- ─── Free oscillation: homogeneous ODE ─── -->

  <remark xml:id="rem-harmonic-solution-strategy">
    <title>Solution Strategy</title>
    <p>
      The harmonic oscillator is the concrete place where the abstract theory
      of this section comes together.  First we solve the homogeneous equation
      (<m>F=0</m>) to understand the system's free motion.  Then we add a
      particular solution for the forced case (<m>F \neq 0</m>), exactly as in
      <xref ref="alg-undetermined-coefficients" text="title"/>, to describe how an external
      excitation changes that motion.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <!-- ─── Case 1a: undamped ─── -->

  <theorem xml:id="thm-undamped-free-oscillation">
    <title>Case 1a — Free Undamped Oscillation (<m>\beta = 0</m>)</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        With no damping (<m>\beta = 0</m>), equation
        <xref ref="eq-harmonic-oscillator"/> with <m>F = 0</m> reduces to
        <me>
          y''(t) + \omega_0^2\, y(t) = 0.
        </me>
        The characteristic polynomial <m>P(\lambda) = \lambda^2 + \omega_0^2</m>
        has the purely imaginary roots <m>\lambda_{1,2} = \pm i\omega_0</m>.
        The general solution is
        <me>
          y(t) = C_1 \cos(\omega_0 t) + C_2 \sin(\omega_0 t).
        </me>
        The system oscillates at frequency <m>\omega_0</m>
        with constant amplitude — no decay, no growth.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

  <figure xml:id="fig-undamped-oscillation">
    <caption>
      Free undamped oscillation: a pure sinusoid at frequency
      <m>\omega_0</m> with period <m>T = 2\pi/\omega_0</m>.
      The amplitude never changes.
    </caption>
    <image xml:id="image-undamped-oscillation" width="65%">
      <latex-image>
        \begin{tikzpicture}
          \begin{axis}[
            width=9cm, height=5cm,
            xlabel={$t$}, ylabel={$y(t)$},
            xmin=0, xmax=6.5, ymin=-1.5, ymax=1.8,
            axis lines=left,
            xtick={3.14159, 6.28318},
            xticklabels={$\pi/\omega_0$, $2\pi/\omega_0$},
            ytick={-1,0,1},
            samples=200, smooth
          ]
            \addplot[thick, blue, domain=0:6.5] {cos(deg(x))};
            \draw[dashed, gray] (axis cs:0,1) -- (axis cs:6.5,1);
            \draw[dashed, gray] (axis cs:0,-1) -- (axis cs:6.5,-1);
            \draw[latex-latex, gray!70!black] (axis cs:0,1.4) -- (axis cs:6.28,1.4);
            \node[above] at (axis cs:3.14,1.4) {\small$T = 2\pi/\omega_0$};
          \end{axis}
        \end{tikzpicture}
      </latex-image>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <!-- ─── Case 1b: damped ─── -->

  <theorem xml:id="thm-damped-free-oscillation">
    <title>Case 1b — Free Damped Oscillation (<m>\beta > 0</m>)</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        With positive damping, the characteristic polynomial
        <m>P(\lambda) = \lambda^2 + 2\beta\lambda + \omega_0^2</m>
        has discriminant <m>\Delta = \beta^2 - \omega_0^2</m>.
        Three qualitatively different behaviours arise.
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Case 1b.i — Underdamped (<m>\beta^2 \lt \omega_0^2</m>).</em>
        Roots: <m>\lambda_{1,2} = -\beta \pm i\omega_d</m> where
        <m>\omega_d = \sqrt{\omega_0^2 - \beta^2} > 0</m>
        is the <term>damped natural frequency</term>.
        General solution:
        <me>
          y(t) = e^{-\beta t}\!\left(C_1\cos(\omega_d t)
                 + C_2\sin(\omega_d t)\right).
        </me>
        The system oscillates with exponentially decreasing amplitude.
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Case 1b.ii — Critically damped (<m>\beta^2 = \omega_0^2</m>).</em>
        Double root <m>\lambda_0 = -\beta</m>.
        General solution:
        <me>
          y(t) = C_1\, e^{-\beta t} + C_2\, t\, e^{-\beta t}.
        </me>
        The system returns to rest as fast as possible without
        oscillating — the <em>aperiodic limit case</em>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Case 1b.iii — Overdamped (<m>\beta^2 > \omega_0^2</m>).</em>
        Two distinct real roots
        <m>\lambda_{1,2} = -\beta \pm \sqrt{\beta^2 - \omega_0^2}</m>
        (both negative, since <m>\sqrt{\beta^2 - \omega_0^2} \lt \beta</m>).
        General solution:
        <me>
          y(t) = C_1\, e^{\lambda_1 t} + C_2\, e^{\lambda_2 t}.
        </me>
        The system decays to rest without oscillating,
        but more slowly than in the critically damped case.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

  <figure xml:id="fig-damped-oscillation-cases">
    <caption>
      The three qualitatively different responses of a free damped
      harmonic oscillator, plotted for
      <m>\omega_0 = 1</m> and initial conditions <m>y(0) = 1</m>,
      <m>y'(0) = 0</m>.
      Underdamped (<m>\beta = 0.2</m>): oscillatory decay.
      Critically damped (<m>\beta = 1</m>): fastest non-oscillatory return.
      Overdamped (<m>\beta = 1.5</m>): slow non-oscillatory decay.
    </caption>
    <image xml:id="image-damped-oscillation-cases" width="70%">
      <latex-image>
        \begin{tikzpicture}
          \begin{axis}[
            width=10cm, height=6cm,
            xlabel={$t$}, ylabel={$y(t)$},
            xmin=0, xmax=10, ymin=-0.6, ymax=1.1,
            axis lines=left,
            ytick={0,1},
            xtick={0,2,4,6,8,10},
            legend pos=north east,
            legend style={font=\small},
            samples=300, smooth
          ]
            % Underdamped: beta=0.2, omega_0=1, omega_d=sqrt(1-0.04)=0.98
            \addplot[thick, blue, domain=0:10]
              {exp(-0.2*x)*(cos(deg(0.98*x))
               + (0.2/0.98)*sin(deg(0.98*x)))};
            \addlegendentry{Underdamped ($\beta{=}0.2$)}
            % Critically damped: beta=1, y=(1+t)e^{-t}
            \addplot[thick, red!80!black, domain=0:10]
              {(1+x)*exp(-x)};
            \addlegendentry{Critical ($\beta{=}1$)}
            % Overdamped: beta=1.5, lambda1=-0.268, lambda2=-2.732
            % y(0)=1, y'(0)=0 => C1+C2=1, -0.268C1-2.732C2=0
            % C2 = -0.268/(2.464) * C1 ... let me compute properly
            % lambda1 = -1.5 + sqrt(2.25-1) = -1.5+1.118=-0.382
            % lambda2 = -1.5 - 1.118 = -2.618
            % C1+C2=1; -0.382 C1 - 2.618 C2 = 0 => C1 = 2.618 C2 / 0.382 = 6.85 C2
            % 6.85 C2 + C2 = 1 => C2 = 0.127, C1 = 0.873
            \addplot[thick, green!60!black, domain=0:10]
              {0.873*exp(-0.382*x) + 0.127*exp(-2.618*x)};
            \addlegendentry{Overdamped ($\beta{=}1.5$)}
            \draw[dashed, gray] (axis cs:0,0) -- (axis cs:10,0);
          \end{axis}
        \end{tikzpicture}
      </latex-image>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <!-- ─── Case 2: forced oscillation and resonance ─── -->

  <theorem xml:id="thm-forced-oscillation">
    <title>Case 2 — Forced Oscillation and Resonance</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Now consider an external sinusoidal force
        <m>F(t) = F_0 \sin(\omega t)</m> applied to a damped oscillator
        (<m>\beta \geq 0</m>).
        Using the method of undetermined coefficients with trial function
        <m>y_p(t) = B_1\cos(\omega t) + B_2\sin(\omega t)</m>
        (valid whenever <m>\beta > 0</m> or <m>\omega \neq \omega_0</m>),
        substitution into <xref ref="eq-harmonic-oscillator"/> gives
        <md>
          <mrow>
            (\omega_0^2 - \omega^2) B_1 + 2\beta\omega B_2 \amp= 0,
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            -2\beta\omega B_1 + (\omega_0^2 - \omega^2) B_2
            \amp= \frac{F_0}{m}.
          </mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
      <!-- ADDED: short coefficient derivation makes the amplitude and phase formulas transparent -->
      <p>
        Solving this linear system yields
        <md>
          <mrow>
            B_1 \amp=
            -\frac{F_0}{m}\,
            \frac{2\beta\omega}{(\omega_0^2 - \omega^2)^2 + 4\beta^2\omega^2},
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            B_2 \amp=
            \frac{F_0}{m}\,
            \frac{\omega_0^2 - \omega^2}{(\omega_0^2 - \omega^2)^2 + 4\beta^2\omega^2}.
          </mrow>
        </md>
        Hence the particular solution can be rewritten in amplitude-phase form as
        <me>
          y_p(t)
          = \frac{F_0/m}{\sqrt{(\omega_0^2 - \omega^2)^2 + 4\beta^2\omega^2}}
            \sin\!\bigl(\omega t - \varphi(\omega)\bigr),
        </me>
        where the <term>phase shift</term> <m>\varphi(\omega)</m> satisfies
        <m>\tan\varphi = 2\beta\omega/(\omega_0^2 - \omega^2)</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The <term>amplitude of the steady-state response</term> is
        <me>
          A(\omega)
          = \frac{F_0/m}{\sqrt{(\omega_0^2 - \omega^2)^2 + 4\beta^2\omega^2}}.
        </me>
        When <m>\beta</m> is small and <m>\omega \approx \omega_0</m>,
        the denominator is small and <m>A(\omega)</m> becomes very large —
        this is the <term>resonance</term> phenomenon.
      </p>
      <p>
        In the undamped case <m>\beta = 0</m> with driving frequency
        <m>\omega = \omega_0</m>, the method of undetermined coefficients
        requires the resonance trial function
        <m>y_p(t) = t\bigl(B_1\cos(\omega_0 t) + B_2\sin(\omega_0 t)\bigr)</m>,
        and the amplitude grows linearly without bound.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

  <figure xml:id="fig-resonance-amplitude-curve">
    <caption>
      Frequency response of the amplitude <m>A(\omega)</m> for fixed
      <m>F_0 = m = 1</m>, <m>\omega_0 = 1</m> and several damping values.
      As <m>\beta \to 0</m> the resonance peak at <m>\omega \approx \omega_0</m>
      becomes sharper and taller; for <m>\beta = 0</m> it diverges.
    </caption>
    <image xml:id="image-resonance-amplitude-curve" width="70%">
      <latex-image>
        \begin{tikzpicture}
          \begin{axis}[
            width=10cm, height=6cm,
            xlabel={Driving frequency $\omega$},
            ylabel={Amplitude $A(\omega)$},
            xmin=0, xmax=3, ymin=0, ymax=6.5,
            axis lines=left,
            ytick={0,1,2,3,4,5,6},
            xtick={0,0.5,1,1.5,2,2.5,3},
            xticklabels={$0$, , $\omega_0$, , $2\omega_0$, , $3\omega_0$},
            legend pos=north east,
            legend style={font=\small},
            samples=300, smooth, thick
          ]
            % beta=0.1
            \addplot[blue, domain=0:0.85, restrict y to domain=0:6.5]
              {1/sqrt((1-x^2)^2 + 4*0.01*x^2)};
            \addplot[blue, domain=0.88:3, restrict y to domain=0:6.5]
              {1/sqrt((1-x^2)^2 + 4*0.01*x^2)};
            \addlegendentry{$\beta=0.1$}
            % beta=0.3
            \addplot[red!70!black, domain=0:3, restrict y to domain=0:6.5]
              {1/sqrt((1-x^2)^2 + 4*0.09*x^2)};
            \addlegendentry{$\beta=0.3$}
            % beta=0.7
            \addplot[green!60!black, domain=0:3, restrict y to domain=0:6.5]
              {1/sqrt((1-x^2)^2 + 4*0.49*x^2)};
            \addlegendentry{$\beta=0.7$}
            % beta=1 (overdamped - no peak)
            \addplot[orange, domain=0:3, restrict y to domain=0:6.5]
              {1/sqrt((1-x^2)^2 + 4*1*x^2)};
            \addlegendentry{$\beta=1.0$}
            % Mark omega_0
            \draw[dashed, gray] (axis cs:1,0) -- (axis cs:1,6.5);
          \end{axis}
        \end{tikzpicture}
      </latex-image>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <remark xml:id="rem-reading-frequency-response">
    <title>How to Read the Frequency-Response Graph</title>
    <p>
      For a fixed damping value <m>\beta</m>, the graph of <m>A(\omega)</m>
      tells us which driving frequencies are dangerous: tall peaks mean that a
      small periodic force can produce a large steady-state oscillation.
    </p>
    <p>
      The peak is highest near <m>\omega = \omega_0</m> when damping is weak.
      Increasing damping lowers and broadens the peak, which is why damping is
      an effective engineering tool for suppressing resonance.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <remark xml:id="rem-resonance-catastrophe">
    <title>Resonance in Engineering: The Tacoma Narrows Bridge</title>
    <p>
      Resonance is not merely a mathematical curiosity.
      When an external excitation is close to the natural frequency of a
      structure, even moderate forcing can produce catastrophically large
      oscillations.
      A famous historical example is the Tacoma Narrows Bridge
      (Washington State, USA, 1940), which collapsed just four months after
      opening due to aerodynamic forces that matched the bridge's natural
      frequency.
      Modern structural engineering avoids resonance by careful tuning of
      natural frequencies away from expected excitation spectra, and by
      deliberately introducing damping (e.g.\ tuned mass dampers in
      skyscrapers).
    </p>
  </remark>

  <exercises xml:id="exercises-harmonic-oscillator-check">
    <title>Check Your Understanding</title>

    <exercise xml:id="exer-harmonic-resonance-concept">
      <title>Recognise Resonance</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          When is resonance most likely to occur in a forced oscillator?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p>When the driving frequency is close to the natural frequency and damping is small</p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Correct. Then the steady-state amplitude can become very large.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>When the damping is extremely large</p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Incorrect. Strong damping suppresses resonance peaks.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>Whenever the forcing term is zero</p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Incorrect. With no forcing, there is no forced resonance.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>Only when the natural frequency is zero</p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Incorrect. Resonance is tied to matching frequencies, not to a zero natural frequency.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="exer-harmonic-undamped-general-solution">
      <title>Free Undamped Oscillation</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Solve the homogeneous ODE
          <me>
            y''+4y=0.
          </me>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The characteristic equation is
          <me>
            \lambda^2+4=0,
          </me>
          so the roots are <m>\lambda=\pm 2i</m>. Therefore the general solution is
          <me>
            y(t)=C_1\cos(2t)+C_2\sin(2t),
            \qquad C_1,C_2\in\R.
          </me>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
  </exercises>

</subsection>
